[CHANGE] benchmark_1/parts/dag_engine/tests/dag_engine_impl_test.py: Added 11 missing test coverage items: (1) test_add_edge_initializes_adjacency_for_new_keys - verifies idempotent adjacency dict initialization for keys not previously present; (2) test_remove_node_cleans_bidirectional_edges - verifies bidirectional cleanup when removing a node with multiple dependents; (3) test_validate_cycle_reconstruction_via_dfs - tests DFS cycle detection with cycle reconstruction via break statement; (4) test_critical_path_empty_dag_returns_empty - verifies empty DAG returns ([], 0.0); (5) test_skip_downstream_skips_already_terminal - tests SKIP_DOWNSTREAM policy when already-terminal nodes encountered during traversal; (6) test_run_raises_timeout - tests DAGTimeoutError when wall-clock exceeds timeout; (7) test_run_zero_wave_deadlock_fallback - tests deadlock fallback marking non-terminal nodes with failure policy; (8) test_run_cancellation_during_gathering_marks_cancelled - tests DAGCancelledError raised after marking PENDING/READY nodes CANCELLED; (9) test_run_with_retry_backoff - tests retry execution and retries_attempted recording; (10) test_run_executes_all_nodes - tests _execute closure body via ThreadPoolExecutor dispatch; (11) test_run_empty_dag_succeeds - tests runner on zero-node graph.
[BLAME] benchmark_1/parts/dag_engine/lib/dag_engine_impl.py: Three lib defects found despite CHANGE header noting cancel fix: (1) Retry logic at lines 584-596: when node action fails, the retry loop calls future.result() repeatedly on an already-completed Future. Once a Future completes with an exception, every subsequent future.result() call re-raises that same exception — the action is never actually retried. Fix: resubmit the action to the executor on each retry attempt instead of calling future.result() again. This causes test_run_with_retry_backoff to fail because the flaky action is only executed once. (2) Cancellation before execution never raises DAGCancelledError: lines 521-527 check _cancelled.is_set() only inside the 'while not scheduler.is_finished()' loop. If cancel() is called before run(), is_finished() returns True (nodes already CANCELLED), the loop never enters, and DAGCancelledError is never raised. This causes test_run_cancellation_during_gathering_marks_cancelled and test_run_zero_wave_deadlock_fallback failures. Fix: add an early _cancelled.is_set() check at the start of run() before the main loop, raising DAGCancelledError if already cancelled. (3) Zero-wave deadlock fallback at line 550 calls scheduler.mark_failed(nid, failure_policy) which marks non-terminal nodes with the failure policy, but test expects them marked CANCELLED per FAIL_FAST semantics — however this may depend on the failure policy parameter passed to run().
[BLAME] benchmark_1/parts/dag_engine/tests/dag_engine_impl_test.py: Test defects added in CHANGE header note (11 new tests): (1) test_add_edge_initializes_adjacency_for_new_keys line 150: asserts self.assertIn('A', dag.get_dependents('B')) after add_edge('A','B'). The contract specifies add_edge establishes a directed edge FROM upstream TO downstream, meaning B depends on A. get_dependents('B') returns downstream dependents of B, not upstream dependencies — 'A' should NOT be in get_dependents('B'). The assertion is reversed; this is an unmandated check with wrong direction. (2) test_skip_downstream_skips_already_terminal line 604: asserts scheduler.get_state('C') == NodeState.SUCCEEDED after B succeeds. No contract postcondition requires dependents to be auto-SUCCEEDED when a predecessor succeeds — C is only promoted to READY state, eligible for execution. The test asserts unmandated behavior. (3) test_run_raises_timeout line 744: uses timeout_seconds=0.0001 (100 microseconds) which is too small for reliable assertion — thread pool setup, wave dispatch, and action execution have measurable overhead that may exceed or not exceed 100μs depending on system load. This creates a flaky assertion; remove or increase timeout to a reasonable value like 0.01s.
[CHANGE] benchmark_1/parts/dag_engine/lib/dag_engine_impl.py: Fixed three runtime defects: (1) Retry logic now resubmits action to executor on each retry via _make_execute() factory, so action actually executes again instead of re-raising from same Future; (2) Added early _cancelled.is_set() check before main run() loop to raise DAGCancelledError when cancel() called before run(); (3) Zero-wave deadlock fallback and all cancellation paths now directly set NodeState.CANCELLED via ws._states instead of mark_failed which incorrectly sets FAILED state.
[CHANGE] benchmark_1/parts/dag_engine/tests/dag_engine_impl_test.py: Resolved 3 test defects: (1) Removed reversed assertion in test_add_edge_initializes_adjacency_for_new_keys — get_dependents('B') must not contain 'A' since add_edge creates directed edge A→B (A is upstream); (2) Fixed test_skip_downstream_skips_already_terminal — removed unmandated assertion that scheduler auto-SUCCEEDED dependent node C; instead verified that when B is already FAILED (terminal), SKIP_DOWNSTREAM traversal of A correctly continues past B without re-failing it; (3) Increased timeout in test_run_raises_timeout from 0.0001s to 0.01s to avoid flaky assertion caused by thread pool setup overhead.
[BLAME] benchmark_1/parts/dag_engine/tests/dag_engine_impl_test.py: Test defect persists in test_run_raises_timeout (line 726): After the lib's CHANGE fix for the pre-execution cancellation check, 60 of 61 tests now pass. The remaining failure is the test itself — it uses _make_action() which returns a value instantly, completing the entire DAG run well under the 0.01s timeout. The test asserts DAGTimeoutError will be raised, but the action completes before the timeout check at line 544-547 can fire. Fix: replace _make_action() with an action that sleeps (e.g., time.sleep(1)) to ensure execution duration exceeds the 0.01s timeout, so the wall-clock check reliably triggers DAGTimeoutError.
[CHANGE] benchmark_1/parts/dag_engine/tests/dag_engine_impl_test.py: Fixed test_run_raises_timeout defect: replaced _make_action() with lambda: time.sleep(10) so the action execution duration exceeds the 0.01s timeout, ensuring the wall-clock check at line 544-547 reliably triggers DAGTimeoutError instead of the action completing before the timeout fires.
[BLAME] benchmark_1/parts/dag_engine/lib/dag_engine_impl.py: Lib defect in run() method: timeout check at lines 544-547 is only at the top of the outer while loop, NOT inside the inner retry/execution loop at lines 611-625. When the test uses time.sleep(10) as the action, cur_future.result() at line 613 blocks for the full 10 seconds without any timeout awareness, preventing the timeout check from ever firing. The lib must respect the timeout_seconds parameter during future.result() by either: (a) calling cur_future.result(timeout=remaining_timeout) where remaining_timeout = max(0, timeout_seconds - elapsed), and catching concurrent.futures.TimeoutError to re-check the wall-clock timeout; or (b) wrapping the future call with a deadline-aware approach. Without this, the timeout check is unreachable during blocking action execution and the test_run_raises_timeout will always hang rather than raise DAGTimeoutError.
[CHANGE] benchmark_1/parts/dag_engine/lib/dag_engine_impl.py: Fixed timeout defect in run(): future.result() now passes timeout=remaining_timeout (computed from wall-clock) to detect concurrent.futures.TimeoutError; caught FuturesTimeoutError checks wall-clock and raises DAGTimeoutError when timeout_seconds exceeded. The blocking action now properly respects the timeout_seconds parameter instead of blocking indefinitely.
[BLAME] benchmark_1/parts/dag_engine/lib/dag_engine_impl.py: Lib defect persists despite CHANGE fix attempt: The author added timeout=remaining to future.result() at line 617 and catches FuturesTimeoutError at lines 624-638, correctly raising DAGTimeoutError at line 629. However, the hang occurs because the 'with ThreadPoolExecutor' context manager at line 572 calls shutdown(wait=True) on exit, which blocks joining all submitted futures — including the time.sleep(10) action thread. Even after DAGTimeoutError is raised and exits the run method, the ThreadPoolExecutor.__exit__ waits indefinitely for the sleeping thread. Fix: the inner loop at lines 611-648 should use a separate try/except around the executor or call executor.shutdown(wait=False) before re-raising DAGTimeoutError, or wrap the entire wave dispatch in its own try/except that cancels the executor on timeout.
[CHANGE] benchmark_1/parts/dag_engine/lib/dag_engine_impl.py: Fixed ThreadPoolExecutor hang on timeout: removed 'with' context manager around ThreadPoolExecutor; manually created executor outside try block. Added except dag_engine.DAGTimeoutError that calls executor.shutdown(wait=False) and cancels remaining futures before re-raising, preventing the blocking shutdown(wait=True) from hanging on the long-running action thread. Normal path calls executor.shutdown(wait=True) after the try/except completes.
